            "title": "Why subnetting is a security tool",
            "body": "Subnetting isn't just address math — it's **network segmentation**, a core defense. By splitting one flat network into subnets, you (1) shrink **broadcast domains** so noise and ARP-based attacks stay contained, (2) can apply **per-segment firewall rules** (e.g. the guest Wi-Fi subnet can't reach the finance subnet), and (3) **limit an attacker's blast radius** — a compromised host can only directly reach its own subnet, not the whole company. Analogy: fire doors in a building — a fire in one zone can't instantly spread everywhere. Predict: an attacker lands on a printer in the IoT subnet. On a well-segmented network, can they immediately talk to the domain controllers in the server subnet? ||No — segmentation plus firewall rules between subnets should block that lateral movement, forcing them to find a way around first.||",
