            "body": "You type `https://example.com` and hit Enter. In under a second, four distinct phases happen in sequence: (1) **DNS resolution** turns the name into an IP, (2) a **TCP handshake** opens a reliable connection to that IP, (3) a **TLS handshake** encrypts the channel (for HTTPS), and (4) an **HTTP request/response** actually fetches the page, which the browser then **renders**. Analogy: looking up a friend's address in a contacts app (DNS), calling to confirm they're home (TCP), agreeing on a secret code so no one eavesdrops (TLS), then finally asking your question and getting the answer (HTTP). Each phase is a place an attacker can interfere and a defender must protect. We'll walk through each.",
