        "moduleId": "m3-linux",
        "number": 29,
        "title": "File Permissions (chmod, chown, chgrp, SUID, SGID, Sticky Bit)",
        "type": "theory",
        "week": 3,
        "description": "Every file/directory has owner/group/other permission triads viewable via `ls -l` (e.g. `-rwxr-xr--`) — the first character shows file type (`-` file, `d` directory), followed by three characters each for owner, group, and other permissions. Misconfigured permissions, especially SUID binaries, are a classic Linux privilege-escalation vector: an SUID binary executes with the privileges of its owner (often root) regardless of who runs it.",
        "keyPoints": [
          "Octal notation: 4=read, 2=write, 1=execute (sum to combine), e.g. `chmod 700 file` (owner: all, group/others: none), `chmod 400 file` (owner: read-only, no one else can access it), `chmod 744 file` (owner: all, group/others: read only)",
          "Permission table: 0=--- 1=--x 2=-w- 3=-wx 4=r-- 5=r-x 6=rw- 7=rwx",
          "chown changes the owning user (typically root-only); chgrp changes the owning group; a user can belong to multiple groups at once (check with `id`)",
          "SUID (chmod u+s / 4xxx): binary runs as its owner regardless of who executes it",
          "SGID (chmod g+s / 2xxx): binary/directory runs as/inherits its owning group",
          "Sticky bit (chmod +t / 1xxx): restricts delete/rename to the file's owner or root — this is exactly why /tmp uses it, so one user can't delete another user's temp files",
          "`find / -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null` lists every SUID-root binary on a box — the first move in manual Linux privilege-escalation enumeration"
        ],
        "terminalCommands": [
          "ls -l",
          "chown user file",
          "chgrp group file",
          "chmod u+x file",
          "chmod 700 file",
          "chmod 400 file",
          "find / -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null"
        ],
        "slides": [
          {
            "title": "Reading a Permission String",
            "body": "Every file or directory has owner/group/other permission triads, viewable via `ls -l` (e.g. `-rwxr-xr--`). The first character shows file type (`-` for file, `d` for directory), followed by three characters each for owner, group, and other permissions.",
            "diagram": {
              "type": "layer-stack",
              "layers": [
                {
                  "label": "Owner",
                  "detail": "rwx — read, write, execute"
                },
                {
                  "label": "Group",
                  "detail": "r-x — read, execute"
                },
                {
                  "label": "Other",
                  "detail": "r-- — read only"
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "In Simple Words",
            "body": "Permissions are just a written list of who is allowed to do what with a file. Write a script and try to run it straight away — `./program.py` — and bash answers `Permission denied`, because `ls -l` shows it as `-rw-rw-r--`: readable and writable, but never executable. Run `chmod +x program.py`, the line becomes `-rwxrwxr-x`, and the same file now runs. The numbers work the same way, just added up: read is 4, write is 2, execute is 1, so the most any single slot can hold is 7 — which is exactly where the name 'octal' comes from."
          },
          {
            "title": "Octal Notation",
            "body": "Permissions can be written as octal digits that sum together: 4=read, 2=write, 1=execute. e.g. `chmod 700 file` gives the owner all permissions and group/others none; `chmod 744 file` gives the owner all permissions and group/others read only. Full table: 0=--- 1=--x 2=-w- 3=-wx 4=r-- 5=r-x 6=rw- 7=rwx."
          },
          {
            "title": "Changing Ownership",
            "body": "chown changes the owning user (typically root-only); chgrp changes the owning group. A user can belong to multiple groups at once — check with `id`."
          },
          {
            "title": "SUID, SGID, and the Sticky Bit",
            "body": "SUID (`chmod u+s` / 4xxx) makes a binary run as its owner regardless of who executes it. SGID (`chmod g+s` / 2xxx) makes a binary or directory run as, or inherit, its owning group. The sticky bit (`chmod +t` / 1xxx) restricts delete/rename to the file's owner or root — exactly why /tmp uses it, so one user can't delete another user's temp files.",
            "diagram": {
              "type": "comparison",
              "columns": [
                {
                  "label": "SUID",
                  "points": [
                    "chmod u+s / 4xxx",
                    "Runs as its owner regardless of who executes it"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "label": "SGID",
                  "points": [
                    "chmod g+s / 2xxx",
                    "Runs as / inherits its owning group"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "label": "Sticky Bit",
                  "points": [
                    "chmod +t / 1xxx",
                    "Restricts delete/rename to the owner or root",
                    "Why /tmp uses it"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            "check": {
              "question": "What does the sticky bit (chmod +t) do?",
              "options": [
                "Grants execute permission to all users",
                "Makes a binary inherit its owning group",
                "Makes a binary run as its owner",
                "Restricts delete/rename to the file's owner or root"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 3,
              "explanation": "The slide states the sticky bit restricts delete/rename to the file's owner or root — exactly why /tmp uses it."
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "Hunting SUID Binaries",
            "body": "Misconfigured permissions, especially SUID binaries, are a classic Linux privilege-escalation vector. `find / -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null` lists every SUID-root binary on a box — the first move in manual Linux privilege-escalation enumeration.",
            "diagram": {
              "type": "flow-chain",
              "steps": [
                {
                  "label": "find / -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null",
                  "detail": "lists every SUID-root binary on a box"
                },
                {
                  "label": "Manual privilege-escalation enumeration",
                  "detail": "the first move on a new box"
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "Try It Yourself",
            "body": "These commands cover viewing, changing, and hunting permissions.",
            "diagram": {
              "type": "terminal",
              "prompt": "$",
              "lines": [
                {
                  "command": "ls -l"
                },
                {
                  "command": "chown user file"
                },
                {
                  "command": "chgrp group file"
                },
                {
                  "command": "chmod u+x file"
                },
                {
                  "command": "chmod 700 file"
                },
                {
                  "command": "find / -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null"
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "Why This Matters",
            "body": "Permissions control exactly who can read, write, or execute a file — and misreading them, especially around SUID binaries, is one of the fastest ways to a Linux privilege escalation. `find / -perm -4000` should be one of your first checks on any box."
          }
        ]
