        "moduleId": "m3-windows",
        "number": 43,
        "title": "Windows Registry (regedit, structure, common keys)",
        "type": "theory",
        "week": 4,
        "description": "The **Registry** is Windows' giant central settings database — nearly every configuration, from your wallpaper to which programs start at boot, lives here. Attackers love it because changing one key can make malware survive reboots, and defenders must know where those keys are.",
        "keyPoints": [
          "Root hives: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE (system-wide), HKEY_CURRENT_USER (per-user), HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT, HKEY_USERS",
          "HKLM\\...\\Run and HKCU\\...\\Run keys auto-launch programs at login — a common persistence/malware mechanism",
          "Real incident pattern: a malicious .msi installer modified HKCU\\...\\Run to add a shutdown command, forcing the machine to shut down on every subsequent login",
          "Registry key modifications are only visible in Event Viewer if object-access auditing is enabled — logged under Event ID 4657",
          "regedit provides a GUI; reg query/reg add work from CMD/scripts for automation or forensic scripting"
        ],
        "terminalCommands": [
          "reg query HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run"
        ],
        "slides": [
          {
            "title": "What the Registry is",
            "body": "The **Registry** is a hierarchical database of **keys** (folders) and **values** (settings) that Windows and apps read constantly. Instead of thousands of scattered config files (like Linux's `/etc`), Windows centralises settings here. Analogy: a city's master records office — one building holding every permit, license, and address. You browse it with the GUI **`regedit`**, or query from scripts with `reg query` and `reg add`. Worked example: `reg query \"HKCU\\Control Panel\\Desktop\" /v Wallpaper` reads your current wallpaper path.",
            "check": {
              "question": "The Windows Registry is best described as:",
              "options": [
                "A web browser",
                "A central hierarchical settings database",
                "A text editor",
                "A firewall"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "It's the OS-wide database of keys/values for system and app configuration."
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "The root hives",
            "body": "The Registry's top branches are **hives**. The two to know: **HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE (HKLM)** holds **system-wide** settings for everyone, and **HKEY_CURRENT_USER (HKCU)** holds settings for the **currently logged-in user** only. Others: **HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT** (file associations) and **HKEY_USERS** (all loaded profiles). Analogy: HKLM is the building's master rulebook; HKCU is your personal desk drawer. This split matters: writing to HKLM usually needs **admin**, but HKCU you can edit as a normal user — which is why low-priv malware often persists under HKCU. Question: non-admin malware wants persistence — which hive can it write without elevation? ||HKCU — the current user's hive is writable without admin rights, unlike HKLM.||",
            "check": {
              "question": "Which hive holds settings for only the logged-in user?",
              "options": [
                "HKLM",
                "HKCU",
                "HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT",
                "HKEY_USERS"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "HKCU = HKEY_CURRENT_USER, per-user; HKLM is system-wide."
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "The Run keys: autostart = persistence",
            "body": "The classic **persistence** mechanism is the **Run key**: anything under `HKLM\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run` or the `HKCU\\...\\Run` equivalent launches automatically at login. Legit apps use it (updaters, cloud sync); so does malware. Worked example: `reg add \"HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run\" /v Updater /d \"C:\\Users\\Public\\evil.exe\"` makes `evil.exe` run every time that user logs in. Analogy: slipping your name onto the 'open the shop each morning' rota so you're always let in. Defenders enumerate Run keys first when hunting persistence.",
            "diagram": {
              "type": "flow-chain",
              "steps": [
                {
                  "label": "User runs malicious .msi",
                  "detail": "e.g. Totally_Legit_Software.msi"
                },
                {
                  "label": "Installer writes HKCU\\...\\Run",
                  "detail": "Adds a value pointing at the payload"
                },
                {
                  "label": "User logs in",
                  "detail": "Windows reads the Run key"
                },
                {
                  "label": "Payload executes",
                  "detail": "e.g. shutdown /s runs"
                },
                {
                  "label": "Repeats every login",
                  "detail": "Persistence achieved"
                }
              ]
            },
            "check": {
              "question": "What makes the Run key attractive to malware?",
              "options": [
                "It encrypts data",
                "Its contents auto-launch at every login (persistence)",
                "It speeds up the CPU",
                "It hides the taskbar"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "Run-key entries execute at logon, giving malware persistence across reboots."
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "A real incident: the login shutdown",
            "body": "In a training incident, a user ran a trojanised installer named `Totally_Legit_Software.msi`. It silently added a value to `HKCU\\...\\Run` that executed `shutdown /s` — so the machine **powered off immediately after every login**, making it almost unusable. The malicious change was invisible in normal use; it only surfaced when investigators enabled auditing and found **Event ID 4657** (a registry value was modified). Analogy: someone wired the light switch to also cut the building's power — nothing looks wrong until you flip it. Lesson: Run-key persistence is simple, effective, and often the first thing to check on a misbehaving host.",
            "check": {
              "question": "In the incident, where did the malicious MSI add its payload?",
              "options": [
                "The BIOS",
                "HKCU\\...\\Run",
                "A Word document",
                "The firewall"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "It wrote a shutdown command to the HKCU Run key, firing at every login."
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "Querying and editing from the CLI",
            "body": "The GUI **`regedit`** is fine for browsing, but operators script with **`reg`**. `reg query <key> /v <value>` reads; `reg add <key> /v <name> /d <data>` writes; `reg delete <key> /v <name>` removes. Worked example (hunting persistence fast): `reg query HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run` dumps every autostart entry for the current user in one shot. Analogy: `regedit` is walking the stacks of the records office; `reg query` is faxing the clerk the exact drawer you want. Why it matters: scripts, remote shells, and forensics all need the non-GUI path.",
            "check": {
              "question": "Which command reads a registry value from a script/CLI?",
              "options": [
                "regedit /v",
                "reg query",
                "icacls",
                "netstat"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "`reg query` reads keys/values non-interactively; `reg add`/`reg delete` modify them."
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "Catching registry abuse",
            "body": "Registry changes are stealthy because, by default, **nothing logs them**. To see modifications you must enable **object-access auditing** (via Group Policy or `auditpol`), after which a changed value generates **Event ID 4657** in the Security log. Defenders also baseline the common **autostart locations** (Run keys, Services, Winlogon, Scheduled Tasks) and alert on new entries. Worked example: after enabling auditing, the login-shutdown malware's write to the Run key produces a 4657 event naming the process that made the change — the smoking gun. Takeaway: the Registry is powerful and quiet; visibility is a choice you must turn on.",
            "check": {
              "question": "Which Event ID records a registry value modification (with auditing on)?",
              "options": [
                "4624",
                "4657",
                "1074",
                "11707"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "4657 = registry value modified; it only logs if object-access auditing is enabled."
            }
          }
        ]
