        "slides": [
          {
            "title": "Processes vs services",
            "body": "A **process** is any running instance of a program (each `chrome.exe` tab, `notepad.exe`). A **service** is a special background process registered with the **Service Control Manager (SCM)** so it can start at boot, run with no user logged in, and restart on failure — web servers, antivirus, the print spooler. Analogy: processes are people currently in the building; services are the staff on permanent shift that the manager (SCM) hires, starts, and restarts. Tools split along this line: `tasklist`/`taskkill` for processes, `sc`/`services.msc` for services.",
            "check": {
              "question": "What distinguishes a service from a normal process?",
              "options": [
                "It's always malware",
                "It's SCM-managed: auto-starts at boot, runs without a logged-in user",
                "It can't use the network",
                "It has no name"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "Services are SCM-managed background processes that start at boot independent of login."
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "Listing processes: tasklist",
            "body": "`tasklist` lists running processes with their **PID**, session, and memory. `tasklist /fo table` forces clean table output; `tasklist /svc` maps each process to the **services** inside it (crucial — many services share one `svchost.exe`). Remotely, `tasklist /s <host> /u <user> /p <pass>` queries another machine — handy when triaging a box you can't sit at. Worked example: an unexpected `powershell.exe` whose parent is `winword.exe` screams **malicious macro**. Analogy: `tasklist` is the building's live occupancy list; `/svc` says which role each person fills. PowerShell's `Get-Process` is the richer, object-based equivalent.",
            "check": {
              "question": "What does `tasklist /svc` add?",
              "options": [
                "Nothing",
                "Which services are hosted in each process",
                "The weather",
                "File permissions"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "/svc maps processes (esp. svchost) to the services they host."
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "Killing processes: taskkill",
            "body": "`taskkill` ends processes. By name: `taskkill /IM notepad.exe`. By PID: `taskkill /PID 1234 /F` (`/F` = force). Multiple: `taskkill /PID 1234 /PID 5678 /F`. The big hammer: `taskkill /F /T /IM <name>` force-kills the process **and its entire child-process tree** (`/T`) — vital when malware spawns helpers that would respawn it. Analogy: `/T` evicts not just the troublemaker but everyone they brought. Worked example: ransomware spawning children → `taskkill /F /T /IM evil.exe` takes down the whole family at once. Question: a process keeps respawning after you kill it alone — what flag helps? ||`/T` — it kills the child-process tree too, breaking a parent/child respawn loop.||",
            "check": {
              "question": "What does `/T` do in taskkill?",
              "options": [
                "Times it out",
                "Terminates the process plus its child-process tree",
                "Tags it",
                "Throttles it"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "/T kills the whole tree; /F forces; /IM targets by image name, /PID by id."
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "Managing services: sc and services.msc",
            "body": "**`sc`** is the CLI for the Service Control Manager. `sc query <name>` shows a service's state (`RUNNING`/`STOPPED`); `sc stop`/`sc start` control it; `sc qc <name>` dumps its **config** — the binary path and run-as account. **`services.msc`** is the GUI. Worked example (security-critical): `sc qc` reveals the **BINARY_PATH_NAME**; if that path is **unquoted and contains spaces** (e.g. `C:\\Program Files\\My App\\svc.exe`) Windows may try `C:\\Program.exe` first — a classic **unquoted service path** escalation if a writable folder sits in the way. Analogy: `sc qc` is reading a staffer's full contract. The account a service runs as (often **SYSTEM**) is why services are escalation targets.",
            "check": {
              "question": "Which command shows a service's binary path and run-as account?",
              "options": [
                "sc query",
                "sc qc",
                "taskkill",
                "tasklist /svc"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "`sc qc <svc>` dumps config incl. BINARY_PATH_NAME and the service account."
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "The PowerShell way",
            "body": "PowerShell treats processes and services as **objects** you can filter and act on. `Get-Process -Name notepad` returns just that process; `Get-Service -Name Spooler` shows the spooler's status; `Stop-Process -Id 1234 -Force` and `Stop-Service` act on them. Because they're objects, you can pipe: `Get-Process | Sort-Object CPU -Descending | Select-Object -First 5` shows your top CPU hogs. Analogy: CMD hands you a printed list to eyeball; PowerShell hands you a spreadsheet you can sort and query. Worked example: hunting a resource leak, that one-liner names the culprit instantly — the scriptable version of Task Manager's Performance tab.",
            "diagram": {
              "type": "flow-chain",
              "steps": [
                {
                  "label": "Find it: tasklist",
                  "detail": "Get the PID / image name"
                },
                {
                  "label": "Inspect: tasklist /svc, sc qc",
                  "detail": "Map services, check binary path & account"
                },
                {
                  "label": "Act: taskkill /F /T",
                  "detail": "Force-kill process and its child tree"
                },
                {
                  "label": "Verify: tasklist again",
                  "detail": "Confirm it didn't respawn"
                }
              ]
            },
            "check": {
              "question": "Why is PowerShell's `Get-Process` more powerful than `tasklist`?",
              "options": [
                "It's colorful",
                "It returns objects you can filter/sort/pipe",
                "It needs admin",
                "It's slower"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "Object output lets you Sort/Select/Where and act on results programmatically."
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "Processes in offense and defense",
            "body": "This toolkit is daily triage. **Defenders**: `tasklist /svc` plus checking parent/child relationships and odd paths (`powershell.exe` in `\\Temp`) spots malware; `taskkill /F /T` stops it. **Attackers**: enumerate services (`sc qc`) for weak or unquoted binary paths, and kill or blind security processes. Worked example (defensive catch): a service whose binary lives in `C:\\Users\\Public\\` and runs as SYSTEM is almost certainly malicious persistence. Takeaway: knowing *what's running, as whom, launched by what* is the single most useful situational-awareness skill on a Windows host.",
            "check": {
              "question": "A service running as SYSTEM whose binary is in C:\\Users\\Public is:",
              "options": [
                "Normal",
                "A strong indicator of malicious persistence",
                "A Windows update",
                "A printer"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "Legit services don't live in user-writable public folders; SYSTEM there is a red flag."
            }
          }
        ]
