        "moduleId": "m3-windows",
        "number": 51,
        "title": "BitLocker & Disk Encryption",
        "type": "theory",
        "week": 4,
        "description": "**BitLocker** is Windows' built-in full-disk encryption: it scrambles everything on the drive so a lost or stolen laptop doesn't leak its data. Understanding how it ties the key to the hardware (via the TPM), and why the recovery key is everything, is essential for both protecting data and knowing its limits.",
        "keyPoints": [
          "Uses the TPM (Trusted Platform Module) to seal the encryption key to the specific hardware",
          "Without the recovery key, an encrypted drive's contents are unreadable even if physically removed",
          "Available from Windows 10/11 Pro upward (not Home); Enterprise adds further hardware-based protections like Credential Guard and Device Guard alongside it"
        ],
        "terminalCommands": [
          "manage-bde -status",
          "manage-bde -on C:"
        ],
        "slides": [
          {
            "title": "Data at rest vs data in use",
            "body": "There are two states to protect: **data in use** (while you're logged in, files are decrypted for you) and **data at rest** (the powered-off disk). BitLocker protects **data at rest** — steal the laptop or pull the drive and the raw sectors are unreadable **ciphertext**. It does **not** protect you from malware while you're logged in (the data is decrypted for your session). Analogy: BitLocker is a vault door locked when the shop is closed; it does nothing about a thief you let inside during business hours. Worked example: a stolen BitLockered laptop → the thief sees only encrypted bytes, not your files.",
            "check": {
              "question": "BitLocker primarily protects data in which state?",
              "options": [
                "In use (logged in)",
                "At rest (powered off / stolen drive)",
                "In transit over the network",
                "In RAM"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "Full-disk encryption secures the drive at rest; a logged-in session has data decrypted."
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "The TPM: sealing the key to the hardware",
            "body": "BitLocker's strength comes from the **TPM (Trusted Platform Module)** — a tamper-resistant chip on the motherboard that stores the key and **releases it only if the boot process is unchanged**. Move the drive to another computer, or tamper with the boot files, and the TPM refuses to hand over the key. Analogy: a safe that only opens in its own room, for its own staff, with the original locks untouched — relocate it and it stays shut. Worked example: pulling the SSD and plugging it into another PC yields nothing, because the key never leaves the original machine's TPM. This hardware binding is what defeats 'just move the disk' attacks.",
            "diagram": {
              "type": "flow-chain",
              "steps": [
                {
                  "label": "Power on",
                  "detail": "System begins boot"
                },
                {
                  "label": "TPM measures boot components",
                  "detail": "Firmware, bootloader, etc."
                },
                {
                  "label": "Integrity unchanged?",
                  "detail": "Compare to sealed values"
                },
                {
                  "label": "Yes -> release key",
                  "detail": "OS decrypts the drive transparently"
                },
                {
                  "label": "No/moved -> withhold key",
                  "detail": "Prompt for the 48-digit recovery key"
                }
              ]
            },
            "check": {
              "question": "What does the TPM do for BitLocker?",
              "options": [
                "Stores your files",
                "Seals the encryption key to this hardware/boot state",
                "Speeds up the disk",
                "Connects to the internet"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "The TPM releases the key only on the original, unmodified machine/boot path."
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "The recovery key is everything",
            "body": "If the TPM won't release the key — hardware change, firmware update, moved disk, forgotten PIN — Windows demands the **48-digit recovery key**. Without it, the encrypted data is **permanently unreadable**, even to the owner. That's the whole point (no backdoor), but it means **losing the recovery key = losing the data**. Analogy: the master override code for the vault; lose it and even you can't get in. Worked example: a BIOS update changes the boot measurements → next boot prompts for the recovery key; enterprises store these in **Active Directory / Entra** so IT can retrieve them. Question: TPM withholds the key after a firmware update — what unlocks the drive? ||The 48-digit BitLocker recovery key; without it the data is unrecoverable.||",
            "check": {
              "question": "Losing the BitLocker recovery key (with no TPM release) means:",
              "options": [
                "Nothing, Windows resets it",
                "The data is permanently unreadable",
                "Automatic cloud restore",
                "The firewall opens"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "There's no backdoor; no recovery key and no TPM release = data gone."
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "Who gets BitLocker, and what's beyond it",
            "body": "BitLocker ships from **Windows 10/11 Pro upward** — **Home does not have it** (a headline reason to choose Pro). **Enterprise** builds further hardware protections on top: **Credential Guard** (isolates secrets like password hashes/Kerberos tickets in a virtualized container so credential-theft tools can't read them) and **Device Guard / WDAC** (only approved code runs). Analogy: Pro gives you the vault; Enterprise adds biometric locks and a guarded evidence room. Worked example: on a Home laptop there's no BitLocker at all — a real data-at-rest gap for small businesses who don't realise it.",
            "check": {
              "question": "Which edition is the LOWEST that includes BitLocker?",
              "options": [
                "Home",
                "Pro",
                "Only Server",
                "Only Enterprise"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "BitLocker starts at Pro; Home lacks it. Enterprise adds Credential/Device Guard."
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "Managing BitLocker and its real limits",
            "body": "Manage it via the **Control Panel** applet, PowerShell (`Get-BitLockerVolume`, `Enable-BitLocker`), or `manage-bde` (`manage-bde -status` shows encryption state). Know the limits so you don't over-trust it: it's beaten by a **running, unlocked machine** (data is decrypted), by **sleep** (key may stay in RAM — cold-boot/DMA concerns), and historically by weak configs (TPM-only with no PIN exposes some physical attacks). Analogy: the vault only protects you when it's actually closed and locked. Worked example: `manage-bde -status C:` tells you whether C: is fully encrypted, decrypting, or protection-suspended. Takeaway: BitLocker is excellent against the lost-laptop threat and not a cure-all once an attacker is inside.",
            "check": {
              "question": "BitLocker does NOT protect against:",
              "options": [
                "A stolen powered-off laptop",
                "An attacker on the already-unlocked, logged-in machine",
                "A drive moved to another PC",
                "Someone reading raw disk sectors offline"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "Once unlocked/logged in, data is decrypted; BitLocker targets the at-rest/physical-theft threat."
            }
          }
        ]
