            "title": "The Most Confused Trio",
            "body": "**Encoding**, **encryption**, and **hashing** all produce gibberish-looking output, so beginners assume they're interchangeable. They are not, and mixing them up is the single biggest cause of **OWASP A02:2021 Cryptographic Failures**. Think of three machines that all make 'noise': one is a translator anyone can reverse, one is a locked safe, and one is a paper shredder. Knowing which machine you used decides whether your data is actually protected - not just whether it *looks* scrambled.",
