        "type": "theory",
        "week": 5,
        "description": "Asymmetric cryptography uses a mathematically linked key pair - a public key anyone can use to encrypt and a private key only the owner holds to decrypt - which solves the key-exchange problem because the public key never needs to stay secret. This topic explains RSA and ECC, why the two families are partners rather than rivals, and why every HTTPS padlock depends on this idea.",
