            "title": "The core idea: your input becomes a command",
            "body": "Some apps shell out to the operating system to do work — ping a host, convert an image, look up stock. When the app builds that shell command by concatenating your input, a **shell metacharacter** can end the intended command and start a new one of your choosing. This is **CWE-78, OS Command Injection**. Analogy: you dictate a note to an assistant who will *do* whatever the note says; you dictate 'buy milk; also wire me the company bank balance' and the semicolon turns one errand into two. The shell reads `;`, `&&`, `||`, and `|` as 'and now run this next thing', which is exactly the attacker's foothold.",
