            "body": "The decisive fix is to **avoid invoking a system shell at all**. Use the language's native library instead of shelling out (e.g. read a directory with a file API rather than calling `ls`). When you genuinely must run an external program, call it with an **argument array / parameterised API** (like `subprocess.run([\"ping\", host])`) so the OS receives the program and its arguments separately and **never parses a shell**, rendering `;`, `|`, and friends inert. Add strict **input validation** (an allowlist — e.g. a store ID must be digits only) as defence in depth, and run the process with least privilege. Why does passing arguments as an array defeat the attack? ||Because no shell is involved, so metacharacters are treated as literal argument text, not as command separators.||",
