            "body": "Modern defence is layered. The browser-level control is the **SameSite cookie attribute**: `SameSite=Lax` (now a common default) stops cookies being sent on most cross-site requests, and `Strict` is tighter. The application-level control is a **CSRF token** (a synchronizer token): an unpredictable, per-session value embedded in each form and verified server-side on every state-changing request — and checked on *all* methods, tied to the session. For sensitive actions, **re-authenticate** or require a second factor. Why do SameSite cookies and CSRF tokens complement each other? ||SameSite blocks the cross-site request from carrying the cookie at the browser, while the token ensures that even an allowed request proves it originated from the real site — defence in depth if one layer is misconfigured.||",
