            "title": "Backups, listings, headers, comments",
            "body": "Disclosure comes in many shapes beyond errors. **Directory listing** left enabled shows `/assets/`, `/logs/`, `/backup/` with no index file, exposing everything inside. **Forgotten backup/temp files** like `wp-config.php.old`, `config.php.bak`, or `.env` often contain live credentials and are directly downloadable. **Version headers** such as `Server: Apache/2.4.49` and `X-Powered-By: PHP/7.2.1` advertise exactly what to exploit. And **developer comments** shipped in HTML/JS can leak endpoints, test accounts, or logic. Which of these most often leaks working database credentials? ||A forgotten backup like `config.php.bak` or an exposed `.env` — these frequently hold live DB usernames and passwords in plain text.||",
