        "moduleId": "m10",
        "number": 132,
        "title": "PortSwigger: SSTI Challenge",
        "type": "lab",
        "week": 10,
        "description": "A guided walkthrough of a PortSwigger server-side template injection lab where you identify the templating engine from its behaviour, confirm evaluation with a math payload, and escalate to command execution using the engine-specific RCE syntax. It turns the SSTI detection-to-RCE pipeline into a repeatable method.",
        "keyPoints": [
          "Fuzz the input to identify the templating engine in use from its error behaviour",
          "Confirm evaluation with a math expression payload",
          "Escalate to command execution via the engine-specific RCE syntax"
        ],
        "slides": [
          {
            "title": "Find the injection point",
            "body": "SSTI lives wherever user input is rendered back through a template — a username shown on a profile, a product name, an email greeting, an error message that echoes your input. In the lab, locate a field whose value reappears in the response (for example a message or a product preview). That reflected value is your candidate: if the server builds its template from that input, your payloads will be *evaluated* rather than printed. Start by confirming the value is reflected at all before testing for evaluation.",
            "check": {
              "question": "What kind of input point is a candidate for SSTI?",
              "options": [
                "A field whose value is reflected back through server-side rendering",
                "A field that is never shown again",
                "A purely client-side JavaScript calculation"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 0,
              "explanation": "SSTI requires input to flow into a server-side template, which reflected, server-rendered values can reveal."
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "Fuzz to identify the engine",
            "body": "Before confirming evaluation, provoke errors to learn *which* engine you face. Submit a polyglot like `${{<%[%'\"}}%\\` and read the response. A stack trace or error message often names the engine or library outright (e.g. a Ruby/ERB error, a Jinja2 traceback, a Freemarker exception). Knowing the engine up front is essential because the confirmation and exploitation syntax differ per engine, and firing the wrong family's payload wastes attempts and can trigger WAF noise.",
            "diagram": {
              "type": "flow-chain",
              "steps": [
                {
                  "label": "Send polyglot",
                  "detail": "${{<%[%'\"}}%\\ into the reflected field"
                },
                {
                  "label": "Read error",
                  "detail": "Trace reveals the engine/library"
                },
                {
                  "label": "Identify",
                  "detail": "e.g. Ruby/ERB, Jinja2, Freemarker"
                },
                {
                  "label": "Choose syntax",
                  "detail": "Pick the matching confirm/RCE payloads"
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "Confirm evaluation with math",
            "body": "Now prove the engine executes your input using the identified engine's math syntax. For Jinja2/Twig send `{{7*7}}`; for Freemarker or Java engines send `${7*7}`; for ERB/Ruby send `<%= 7*7 %>`. If the response shows `49`, evaluation is confirmed — your text ran as code. If it shows the literal payload, either the field is not a template sink or you picked the wrong engine syntax; revisit the fingerprinting step. What response confirms you can proceed to RCE? ||A rendered `49` from a `7*7` payload — it proves the engine computed your expression rather than printing it.||",
            "check": {
              "question": "The ERB payload <%= 7*7 %> returns 49. What does this confirm?",
              "options": [
                "The field is client-side only",
                "The Ruby/ERB engine is evaluating your input server-side",
                "There is a SQL injection"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "A computed 49 from ERB syntax confirms server-side evaluation in a Ruby/ERB engine."
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "Escalate to command execution",
            "body": "With the engine confirmed, use its RCE construct. In **ERB/Ruby**, `<%= system(\"id\") %>` runs an OS command; to solve the lab's deletion objective you send `<%= system(\"rm /home/carlos/morale.txt\") %>` (URL-encoded so the payload survives transport). In **Jinja2** you instead walk Python's object graph: `{{config.__class__.__init__.__globals__['os'].popen('id').read()}}`. Submit the engine-appropriate payload in the same reflected field; the server executes the command, and the required file is deleted, solving the lab.",
            "diagram": {
              "type": "comparison",
              "columns": [
                {
                  "label": "ERB (Ruby)",
                  "points": [
                    "Confirm: <%= 7*7 %>",
                    "RCE: <%= system('rm ...') %>",
                    "URL-encode before sending"
                  ],
                  "highlighted": true
                },
                {
                  "label": "Jinja2 (Python)",
                  "points": [
                    "Confirm: {{7*7}}",
                    "RCE: {{config.__class__...os...popen('id')}}",
                    "Walks the object graph to os"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "Why it worked and how to stop it",
            "body": "The lab chained three moves: fingerprint the engine, confirm evaluation with math, and run an engine-specific command — SSTI straight to RCE. It worked because user input became part of the template *source*, not data. The fix is the same as the theory lesson: never construct templates from user input; pass values only as **data** into pre-written templates. If untrusted users must supply templates, use a logic-less engine in a hardened sandbox — but for this app, keeping the user's message a plain variable (`{{ message }}`) instead of splicing it into the template string closes the hole entirely.",
            "check": {
              "question": "What is the root fix for this SSTI lab?",
              "options": [
                "Block the word 'system'",
                "Pass user input as data into a pre-written template, never build the template from it",
                "Use a longer system prompt"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "Keeping user input as template data rather than template source removes the evaluation path that enables RCE."
            }
          }
        ]
