        "slides": [
          {
            "title": "Why hashes exist",
            "body": "Systems should never store your actual password. Instead they store a **hash**: a fixed-length fingerprint produced by a one-way function (like MD5 or SHA-256). The same input always gives the same hash, but you cannot reverse the hash back into the password. Analogy: it is like grinding a specific coffee bean, you can always re-grind that bean and compare the grounds, but you cannot reconstruct the bean from the grounds. So cracking is not 'un-hashing'; it is guessing a password, hashing the guess, and checking if it matches.",
            "check": {
              "question": "A password hash is:",
              "options": [
                "The password encrypted so it can be decrypted later",
                "A one-way fingerprint you can't reverse, only compare against guesses",
                "The password in reverse order",
                "A random number unrelated to the password"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "Hashing is one-way; cracking works by hashing guesses and comparing, not by reversing."
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "Why a GPU, and why not in a VM",
            "body": "Hashing a candidate is a tiny calculation, but cracking means doing it **billions of times**. A **GPU** has thousands of small cores that run those identical calculations in parallel, so it is dramatically faster than a CPU, exactly the kind of work graphics cards are built for. Practical warning: a **virtual machine usually cannot access the host GPU directly**, so Hashcat inside a VM falls back to the CPU and crawls. Run it on bare metal (or with proper GPU passthrough) for real speed. The reference run hit roughly **72,253 kH/s** on a decent GPU.",
            "check": {
              "question": "Why is Hashcat much slower inside a typical VM?",
              "options": [
                "VMs block the network",
                "The VM usually can't access the host GPU, so it falls back to the slower CPU",
                "Hashcat is disabled in VMs",
                "VMs limit disk space"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "Without GPU passthrough a VM uses the CPU, losing the GPU's massive parallelism."
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "The -m flag: hash-type mode",
            "body": "Hashcat needs to know *which* hash algorithm it is attacking, because each produces a different format. The `-m` flag selects the **mode**: `-m 0` is **MD5**, `-m 1400` is **SHA2-256**. How do you pick correctly? Match the hash's format and length against Hashcat's **example_hashes** wiki page rather than guessing, a 32-character hex string is likely MD5, a 64-character hex string is likely SHA-256. Predict: you feed a 64-hex-character hash but set `-m 0`, what happens? ||Hashcat treats it as MD5 and will never match, because it's hashing guesses with the wrong algorithm; no crack results even for an easy password.||",
            "check": {
              "question": "Which Hashcat mode is SHA2-256?",
              "options": [
                "-m 0",
                "-m 100",
                "-m 1400",
                "-m 500"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 2,
              "explanation": "-m 1400 is SHA2-256; -m 0 is MD5. Confirm format/length against the example_hashes page."
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "The -a flag: attack mode",
            "body": "The `-a` flag chooses *how* candidates are generated. `-a 0` is a **straight (dictionary) attack**: it hashes each line of a wordlist exactly as-is, no extra brute-force character generation mixed in, which keeps the run fast and predictable for pure dictionary cracking. Other modes exist (combinator, brute-force/mask, hybrid), but `-a 0` with a good wordlist cracks the overwhelming majority of weak, reused passwords and is where almost everyone starts. The standard lab wordlist is **rockyou.txt**, a real leaked-password list millions of entries long.",
            "check": {
              "question": "What does '-a 0' do in Hashcat?",
              "options": [
                "A pure brute-force of every character combination",
                "A straight dictionary attack, hashing each wordlist entry as-is",
                "Nothing; it disables cracking",
                "Generates a rainbow table"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "-a 0 is the straight/dictionary attack; it tests each wordlist line without extra mangling."
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "A full command and the --username gotcha",
            "body": "A typical run looks like: `hashcat -m 0 -a 0 hashes.txt rockyou.txt`, mode MD5, straight attack, your hash file, your wordlist. One common snag: if each line of `hashes.txt` is `username:hash` instead of a bare hash, add **`--username`** so Hashcat parses and skips the username column instead of treating it as part of the hash. Forget it and every line fails to parse as a valid hash. Rules (via `-r`) can mutate wordlist words (capitalize, append digits) to catch passwords like `Password1!`, trading speed for coverage.",
            "check": {
              "question": "Your hash file lines read 'admin:5f4dcc3b...'. What flag do you add?",
              "options": [
                "--show",
                "--force",
                "--username",
                "-a 3"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 2,
              "explanation": "--username tells Hashcat each line has a username column to parse out, not part of the hash."
            }
          },
          {
            "title": "Reading the output",
            "body": "A successful crack prints the result on its own line as **`hash:plaintext`**, for example `ef92b778bafe771e89245b89ecbc08a44a4e166c06659911881f383d4473e94f:password123`. Below it, a **status block** shows **Recovered** (how many hashes cracked so far), **Progress** (how far through the keyspace), **Speed** (in kH/s, thousands of hashes per second), and **Time.Started / Estimated** (how long until the run finishes the wordlist). Watching Speed and Progress tells you whether a run will finish in seconds or days. A single unsalted hash against rockyou.txt often cracks in **well under a second**.",
            "diagram": {
              "type": "flow-chain",
              "steps": [
                {
                  "label": "Identify hash type",
                  "detail": "Length/format vs example_hashes page → choose -m (0=MD5, 1400=SHA-256)"
                },
                {
                  "label": "Pick attack mode",
                  "detail": "-a 0 straight dictionary with rockyou.txt"
                },
                {
                  "label": "Run Hashcat",
                  "detail": "hashcat -m 0 -a 0 hashes.txt rockyou.txt (+ --username if needed)"
                },
                {
                  "label": "Read hash:plaintext",
                  "detail": "Cracked lines plus Recovered/Progress/Speed status block"
                }
              ]
            },
            "check": {
              "question": "In Hashcat's status block, what does 'Speed' (kH/s) measure?",
              "options": [
                "Network bandwidth to the target",
                "Thousands of hashes computed/tested per second",
                "Number of passwords already found",
                "Disk read speed"
              ],
              "correctIndex": 1,
              "explanation": "kH/s = thousands of hashes per second, your cracking throughput; Recovered counts hits."
            }
          }
        ]
