            "title": "SUID binaries",
            "body": "A **SUID** ('set user ID') binary runs with the privileges of its *owner*, not the user who launches it. So a SUID-root binary runs as **root no matter who executes it**, by design (that's how `passwd` lets you change your own password). The danger is a SUID-root program that can be coaxed into running arbitrary commands. Find them all with **`find / -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null`**. Analogy: a tool stamped 'use my owner's authority', harmless for `passwd`, catastrophic if that tool happens to be one that can run commands.",
