            "title": "Kernel exploits: Dirty COW",
            "body": "When misconfigurations fail, a **vulnerable kernel** itself can be the way up. **Dirty COW** (CVE-2016-5195) is a famous Linux privilege-escalation bug: a **race condition** in the kernel's copy-on-write (COW) memory handling that lets an unprivileged user write to files they should only be able to read, which can be leveraged to gain root. Kernel exploits are powerful but riskier, a wrong one can crash (panic) the box, so they're usually a last resort after misconfig checks. Always confirm the running kernel version (`uname -r`) matches the exploit's affected range before trying it, and never invent a CVE, verify it.",
