            "title": "The standard library: os, random, time",
            "body": "Python ships with a huge **standard library** you pull in using `import`. Three you'll use constantly: `os` talks to the operating system — `os.getcwd()` (current directory), `os.listdir()`, `os.path.exists(path)`, and `os.system(\"...\")` or the safer `subprocess` to run shell commands. `random` adds unpredictability — `random.randint(1, 100)`, `random.choice(mylist)` — useful for jittering request timing to look less robotic. `time` handles timing — `time.time()` gives the current epoch seconds (to measure how long a scan took), and `time.sleep(2)` pauses the script, which you use to avoid hammering a target too fast. Analogy: the standard library is a **well-stocked toolbox that comes free with the drill** — you don't buy the bits separately.",
