            "title": "Reading command-line arguments with argparse",
            "body": "A professional tool takes **flags** so the user configures it without editing code. Python's `argparse` makes this clean. You create a parser and declare each flag: `parser.add_argument(\"-i\", \"--interface\", dest=\"interface\")` and `parser.add_argument(\"-m\", \"--mac\", dest=\"new_mac\")`. A small `get_arguments()` function wraps `parser.parse_args()` and returns the result, so `main()` simply reads `args.interface` and `args.new_mac`. Analogy: `argparse` is the **order form at a counter** — the customer ticks the boxes (`-i eth0 -m 00:11:...`) and the kitchen reads a clean, validated order. It also auto-generates a `--help` message for free.",
