            "title": "Running system commands with subprocess",
            "body": "To actually change the MAC you run three Linux commands, and Python's `subprocess` module is the safe way to do that. `change_mac()` calls them in sequence: bring the interface down `subprocess.run([\"sudo\",\"ip\",\"link\",\"set\",\"dev\",iface,\"down\"], check=True)`, set the new address `... \"link\",\"set\",\"dev\",iface,\"address\",new_mac ...`, then bring it back up `... \"set\",\"dev\",iface,\"up\"`. Passing the command as a **list of arguments** (not one big string) is the secure habit — it avoids shell-injection because the arguments never get re-parsed by a shell. Analogy: handing someone a labelled box of parts is safer than shouting one long sentence they might mishear.",
