            "body": "In a company network you touch dozens of servers a day — file shares, email, databases. **Kerberos** is the protocol **Active Directory** uses so you type your password **once** at login instead of re-sending it to every server. Analogy: at a theme park you show your ticket **once** at the main gate and get a **wristband**; after that every ride just glances at the wristband. Worked example: Alice logs in at 9am and for the rest of the day her access to the file server, print server, and intranet all rides on that one login — the password itself never travels to those servers, only cryptographic **tickets** do. Predict: if the password is never sent to the file server, what does the file server check instead? ||A ticket that was encrypted with a key only the file server and the domain controller share.||",
