            "title": "Impersonating a domain controller",
            "body": "**DCSync** is breathtakingly clean: the attacker **asks a real Domain Controller to send over account password data** — and the DC **complies**, because it thinks it's talking to **another DC** doing normal **replication**. No malware on the DC, no files touched. With the right permissions you can pull **any account's hash**, including **krbtgt** and every Domain Admin. Analogy: you wear a convincing \"HQ courier\" uniform and the branch office hands you the vault's master ledger, no questions asked. Predict: does DCSync require running code on the DC? ||No — it's a legitimate network request made from the attacker's own machine.||",
