            "title": "Automated tools vs doing it by hand",
            "body": "**WinPEAS** (from the PEASS-ng project) and **Seatbelt** (from GhostPack) both automate the exact same checklist: services, scheduled tasks, registry autoruns and policies, installed software and patch level, and stored credentials. Think of them as a metal detector that sweeps the whole beach in seconds, versus digging by hand. The catch: that metal detector is loud. Modern **AV/EDR** frequently flags `winpeas.exe` on sight, and a restrictive shell may not let you upload any binary at all. That is exactly why you memorise the manual commands, because the manual version is your fallback. Predict: which runs quieter, a single built-in `wmic` command or dropping a 2 MB scanner binary? ||The built-in command, because it uses tools already on the box and writes nothing suspicious to disk.||",
