            "title": "Beacon frames: the AP's heartbeat",
            "body": "An access point does not wait to be asked who it is — it constantly announces itself with **beacon frames**, broadcast roughly ten times every second. Each beacon is a little business card carrying the network name, the AP's identity, its channel, and which encryption it supports. Analogy: a lighthouse sweeping its beam every few seconds saying 'I am here, I am the Harbor light, I speak these signals.' Because beacons are broadcast in the clear and unencrypted, monitor mode sees them immediately — which is why a scan populates a list of nearby networks within a second or two. Worked example: idle in a cafe for 10 seconds and a single AP has already emitted roughly ||100 beacon frames (about 10 per second).||",
