            "title": "What WEP was meant to be",
            "body": "**WEP** stands for **Wired Equivalent Privacy**, and the name reveals its modest goal: make wireless *about as private as a wired cable*, where someone would have to physically plug in to snoop. It was the security built into the first consumer WiFi gear around 1999. The ambition was reasonable; the execution had a fatal flaw baked into the math. Analogy: WEP is like a front door that *looks* solid but whose lock can be opened by anyone who watches it being used enough times. You will almost never meet WEP in the field now, but its failure is the reason everything after it works the way it does.",
