            "title": "The fatal flaw: 24 bits is tiny",
            "body": "An IV is only **24 bits**, so there are just 2^24 ≈ **16.7 million** possible values. That sounds like a lot until you count real traffic: a busy network sends thousands of frames per second, so it burns through all 16.7 million IVs in hours and is *forced* to start reusing them. Worked example: at roughly 1,000 frames/sec, you exhaust the entire IV space in under 5 hours, and in practice collisions start far sooner thanks to the birthday effect. Predict: when two frames reuse the same IV with the same static key, what is identical about them? ||They were encrypted with the exact same RC4 keystream — that repetition is the leak an attacker exploits.||",
