            "body": "Put it all together and one conclusion falls out: since attackers can only *guess*, the strength of your passphrase is literally what protects a WPA2 network. A short or common passphrase like `password123` is found in seconds because it sits in every wordlist; a long, random, high-**entropy** passphrase may be computationally infeasible to guess at all. Analogy: the lock design is sound, so the only weakness left is choosing an obvious combination. Worked example: a 5-word random passphrase is effectively unguessable by dictionary, while a pet's name plus a year is trivial. Defender takeaway: WPA2-PSK security is a passphrase-entropy problem, which is also why WPA3 and enterprise auth exist to remove even that risk.",
