            "title": "Cloning the ESSID",
            "body": "The heart of the attack is a **rogue access point** broadcasting the *same ESSID* as a legitimate network — and often the same channel and apparent security type to look authentic. Recall from Topic 1 that the ESSID (network name) can be freely duplicated; nothing stops an attacker from naming their AP `CoffeeShop-WiFi` too. Analogy: a shop that copies a famous brand's exact storefront and sign, counting on customers not to check the fine print. The victim's device sees a familiar name and trusts it. This works precisely because users — and their devices — identify networks by name, a value that carries no guarantee of authenticity.",
